course: dictionary
lesson: 6165
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ming is not an ordinary English word. It is a Chinese word and a name, and it can mean different things depending on where you meet it. The clues around it tell you which meaning you need.
explain: In Mandarin Chinese, «míngtiān» means tomorrow, and «míngzi» means name. Both start with the sound ming, which means bright or clear. Look at the words around it: «míngtiān jiàn» means see you tomorrow, so the word is about time, not light.
explain: In history, Ming is the name of a royal family. «Most of the Great Wall you see in photos today was built or rebuilt by the Ming dynasty.» Walls and rebuilding tell you Ming means a family of rulers, not a word about light.
explain: UK slang has a different word, «minging», which means disgusting. The word «ming» on its own is rarely heard. When the clues do not settle it, check a dictionary.
fact: Ming is a Chinese word and a name, not an ordinary English word.
fact: «Míngtiān» means tomorrow in Mandarin, and «míngzi» means name.
fact: «Míng» means bright or clear in Mandarin Chinese.
fact: The Ming dynasty was a family of emperors who ruled China for nearly three hundred years.
fact: «Minging» is UK slang for disgusting, and «ming» is rarely heard on its own.
exercise: «míngtiān jiàn» means see you tomorrow. What does míngtiān mean? | Yesterday | *Tomorrow | Bright | Name
exercise: «Ming dynasty» is a royal family. What is the Ming dynasty? | A kind of tea from Kenya | A giraffe at court | *A family of emperors who ruled China | A modern football team
exercise: Which word is UK slang for disgusting? | Ming dynasty | Míngtiān | *Minging | Míngzi
test: In Mandarin, «míngzi» means what? | Bright | *Name | Emperor | Tomorrow
test: The Ming dynasty ruled China for nearly how long? | Nearly three weeks | *Nearly three hundred years | Nearly thirty minutes | Nearly three days
exam: Ming can be a Chinese word, a royal family or slang. What should you do when the context is not enough? | *Check a dictionary | Skip the whole text | Guess the first meaning and move on | Ignore the word forever
